\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Leaf security-error terms, with the bound \emph {form} each source actually provides. No term carries a reproduced concrete or parameterised value; the $2^{-191}$ figure is executor-equivalence test coverage, explicitly not a bound on $\epsilon _{\mathrm {AIR}}$. The assembly multiplicities are $N$ on $\epsilon _{\mathrm {EUF}}$, $N_{\mathrm G}$ on $\epsilon _{\mathrm {AIR}}$, and $k+2$ on $\epsilon _{\mathrm {sZK}}$ (once for unlinkability); all are polynomial and do not affect negligibility.}}{116}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Round-margin disclosure. The Griffin instances broken in practice by algebraic (CICO) attacks share our S-box degree but differ in state width, field, and round count. The added \emph {rounds req.}\ column gives the recommended count from Table~2 of~\cite {cryptoeprint:2022/403}: the broken $t{=}12$ instances are attacked at their full recommended count, while our formula-derived $14$ sits one below the $15$ that table lists for our size. We do not claim $14$ rounds establishes a $128$-bit floor. The estimator at our parameters is future work.}}{117}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Property-survival matrix for the credential proof on the studied stack. The bare STARK receipt is feasible ($14.25$\,min, $n{=}3$) and knowledge-sound but not zero-knowledge~\cite {sp1docs-sec,risc0zkadvisory,l2beatzk}, though post-quantum-sound because hash-based. The pairing wraps are zero-knowledge but not post-quantum, and are not produced within budget on the stack as shipped: SP1's PLONK wrap meets the recursion-shape provisioning wall (jetsam at ${\approx }20$\,GB at default concurrency, and a recursive-compression failure at ${\approx }17$\,GB once concurrency is lowered; a ${\approx }51$\,h one-time regeneration, not a memory bound), and RISC~Zero's Groth16 prover is $\texttt {x86}$-only and hence unavailable on the Apple-silicon target~\cite {bruestle2023risczero}. The Groth16 and PLONK feasibility cells are marked \emph {open} rather than infeasible: producing either wrap is a one-time provisioning cost (Horn~1 of Proposition~\ref {prop:wall}), not a memory bound, so the table and the proposition agree that Horn~1 is payable. The bottom-row cell that would carry all four properties is empty on any stack we could run: its feasibility is \emph {open}, not excluded.}}{117}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Evaluation platform. All prove-mode measurements are local single-machine runs with no network or dev-mode acceleration unless stated.}}{118}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces PLUM single-signature verification, prove mode ($\lambda =80$, M5~Pro 24~GB). \emph {Cell~1/2/3} $=$ no precompile / Griffin precompile / SHA-3 control; the control is not a deployable variant (\S \ref {ssec:headline}). $^\dag $Mean of three runs. $^\ddag $Receipt verified.}}{118}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Aurora over a single Loquat-verification R1CS ($\mathbb {F}_{p^2}$, $127$-bit)~\cite {10.1007/978-981-96-0957-4_3}; mean of three runs, range in parentheses. Prover time includes setup, not recompile (\S \ref {ssec:churn-eval}).}}{118}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Same-scheme substrate comparison: one Loquat verification, neither zero-knowledge (three runs per leg). Only the substrate differs; the gap is the zkVM's cost for this scheme. Isolates the substrate for \emph {Loquat}; does not transfer to PLUM.}}{118}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Cycle-level cost attribution (PLUM verification, SHA-3 hasher, $\lambda =128$, execute mode). Routing field arithmetic through the host precompile cuts SP1 and RISC~Zero cycles substantially, RISC~Zero more (heavier baseline emulation).}}{119}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Credential relations ($\lambda =80$). RISC~Zero execute-validates CreGen; its succinct prove yielded no receipt (\S \ref {ssec:cregen-anomaly}). SP1 completes a succinct (non-ZK) prove of both relations, agreeing with the additive cost model. The SP1 CreGen figures of $26.98$ and $27.45$~min ($296$~MB) are the fastest observed, but their shard configuration was recorded only in a human-written summary, not machine-echoed, and does not reproduce on the current fork; the config-verified reproducible completing prove is $70.90$~min ($1.47$~GB) at \texttt {HEIGHT\_THRESHOLD}$=2^{16}$. No completing ShowCre prove has been reproduced on the shipped fork.}}{119}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Proof-mode feasibility matrix. No mode the zkVMs produce on this hardware is dependably zero-knowledge (the dual obstruction); the one ZK proof produced is the static Aurora run. \textsuperscript {$\dagger $}zk-enabled Aurora measured; zk-disabled row kept for comparison. \textsuperscript {$\ddagger $}RISC~Zero receipt not dependably ZK~\cite {risc0zkadvisory,l2beatzk} (\S \ref {sec:security}).}}{120}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Regeneration footprint ($N_{\mathsf {circ}},N_{\mathsf {param}}$; Def.~\ref {def:update-churn}). The static regime regenerates per change; the zkVM absorbs a change as a guest edit, except across the precompile boundary.\textsuperscript {$\ast $} \textsuperscript {$\ast $}Loquat$\to $PLUM regenerates the field-specific Griffin AIR; $N_{\mathsf {src}},N_{\mathsf {audit}}$ in prose.}}{120}{}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Per-change recompile cost: non-preprocessing (Aurora) vs preprocessing (Fractal), same $127$-bit harness. Aurora sub-second; Fractal's indexer OOMs at the BDEC size. The zkVM regenerates no circuit.}}{121}{}%
